## INTRODUCTION
Activity and selectivity in heterogeneous catalysis are highly determined by the structure and chemical properties of the catalyst surface. Typically, methods to enhance the performance of nanocatalysts involve variation of their chemical environment by dispersing them within high surface area supports such as dendrimers, aluminosilicate zeolites, and other porous materials. The catalyst environment in these systems is intrinsically heterogeneous in the spatial arrangement of the chemical functionalities and therefore it is difficult to control their chemistry and catalytic performance. We recently reported a strategy for embedding Pt nanocatalysts in nanocrystalline metal-organic frameworks (nMOFs) and showed that the ordered structure of the MOF leads to significantly higher activity and selectivity in isomerization reactions of methylcyclopentane (MCP) than Pt NPs supported on mesoporous silica used as the reference catalyst. In this report, we take advantage of the facility with which MOFs can be functionalized and show that catalysis by these constructs, Pt⊂nMOFs, is systematically controlled and in some cases enhanced by incorporation of acidic and basic groups, separately or together.
MOFs have been studied with metal NP for their potential application as heterogeneous catalysts. Usually, metal NPs have been introduced by incorporating and reducing metal precursors or by encapsulating them during the synthesis of $\mathrm{\mathop{MOFs}}^{5}$ for catalysis. This study is an example of how metal catalysts can be precisely designed on their exterior and their environment chemically controlled by using MOFs.
